ta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is an array of objects that will be turned into the rows of your table. Each object in the array represents a row of data (under normal circumstances). If you are using TypeScript, we usually define a type for the shape of our data. This type is used as a generic type for all of the other table, column, row, and cell instances. This type is usually referred to as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, if we have a table that displays a list of users in an array like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"age\": 33,\n    \"visits\": 100,\n    \"progress\": 50,\n    \"status\": \"Married\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Vandy\",\n    \"age\": 27,\n    \"visits\": 200,\n    \"progress\": 100,\n    \"status\": \"Single\"\n  }\n]","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then we can define a User (TData) type like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"//TData\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  progress: number\n  status: string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can then define our "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array with this type, and then TanStack Table will be able to intelligently infer lots of types for us later on in our columns, rows, cells, etc."}]},{"type":"code","value":"//note: data needs a \"stable\" reference in order to prevent infinite re-renders\nconst data: User[] = []\n//or\nconst [data, setData] = React.useState<User[]>([])\n//or\nconst data = ref<User[]>([])\n//etc...","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: "},{"type":"inline